.TH NIXCALL 2
.SH NAME
nixsyscall, nixcall, nixret \- system calls through a queue
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <u.h>
.br
.B #include <libc.h>
.br
.B #include <tos.h>
.PP
.B
int	nixsyscall(void)
.PP
.B
int	nixcall(Nixcall *c, int wakekernel)
.PP
.B
void*	nixret(Nixret *r)
.SH DESCRIPTION
A process can make system calls without entering the kernel:
it puts them in the call queue of its
.B Tos
(see
.IR exec (2)),
and a kernel process makes them for it and puts the replies in the
same queue.
A process on an application core
(see
.IR execac (2))
so does I/O without a round trip to a time-sharing core per call.
.PP
.I Nixsyscall
starts the kernel process of the calling process, the first time,
and wakes it when it sleeps.
It must be called before the first
.IR nixcall .
.PP
.I Nixcall
queues the call
.IR c :
.B c->scall
is the number of the system call (see
.BR /sys/src/libc/9syscall/sys.h ),
.B c->arg
its arguments, one 8-byte slot each as the system call stubs pass
them, and
.B c->tag
a value that comes back with the reply; it must not be nil.
If
.I wakekernel
is set and the kernel process sleeps,
.I nixcall
wakes it.
It returns \-1 if the queue is full.
.PP
.I Nixret
returns the tag of the next reply and copies the reply to
.I r
if it is not nil, or returns nil if there is no reply yet.
.B r->sret
is what the system call returned and, if that is \-1,
.B r->err
its error string.
.PP
The kernel process has the user, name space, file descriptors,
environment, current directory and segments of the process (also
those attached later: it takes them again before each batch of
calls); the calls have its rights.
It makes the calls one after another, in their order; a call that
blocks holds up those behind it.
Only calls that make sense from another process are allowed:
.IR open ,
.IR create ,
.IR close ,
.IR pread ,
.IR pwrite ,
.IR seek ,
.IR stat ,
.IR fstat ,
.IR wstat ,
.IR fwstat ,
.IR remove ,
.IR dup ,
.I fd2path
and
.IR nsec ;
the others fail.
The kernel process goes away when the process exits or execs.
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/libc/9sys/nixcall.c
.br
.B /sys/src/9/port/nixcall.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR execac (2)
.SH BUGS
The queue has 64 entries (NIX's had 512).
